Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Skillet Forgives the Driftwood
- Story & Context: When the November blizzard of 1994 severed the communication cables at Kapp Linné, Astrid Dahl stood over an iron woodstove reeking of scorched lard and sputtering tallow while the station's backup diesel generator surged violently. Refusing to freeze in silence, she patched her half-speed ska guitar chops and a rugged SP-1200 boom-bap rhythm directly into the lighthouse's emergency nautophone horn to keep ice from sealing the copper bells shut. As dry rimshots and frozen cowbell hits carried twenty miles out over grinding ice floes, she grabbed the broadcast mic and barked the hook: "Stoke the skillet high, spit the offbeat dry." A fleet of lost crab trawlers picked up the booming broadcast, steering toward the sound and surviving the worst whiteout of the decade.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cinematic 35mm film still of a weathered lighthouse galley during an Arctic storm, a cast-iron skillet smoking over glowing woodstove embers, an antique sampler and brass cowbells resting on the flour-dusted kitchen table, through frosted windowpanes a blinding white snowstorm engulfs dark ocean waves, bathed in warm tungsten amber and icy turquoise tones.

Track 2: Who Salted the Turnip Mush?
- Story & Context: Confined inside the kitchen of Isfjord Radio after a hungry polar bear laid siege to the station porch, Grizz Malmros stirred a bubbling pot of salted turnip mush with a bent tin spoon, nursing a manic cabin fever that rattled his ribs. Unable to step outside to chop firewood, he spent four days hammering an empty brass cowbell and screaming vicious, technical 16-bar verses into a shortwave microphone, venting his terror directly into the maritime frequency. When the Russian cargo vessel Tambov finally broke through the bay ice, the crew listened in total awe over their bridge radio as Grizz’s gravelly voice confessed over dreamy harmonium swells: "Too many spoons in the grease and nobody knows my name." The confession turned a moment of sheer psychological unraveling into the scene's most celebrated street anthem.
