Track 3: Azimuth Broth
- Story & Context: By the spring of 1998, maritime automation had arrived to decommission Slettnes Lighthouse, leaving behind only the cold residue of a farewell lamb-shank broth in an iron kettle on the counter. Station mechanic Torstein Vang refused to board the evacuation helicopter, choosing instead to ski into the tundra after rigging a tape-loop of echoing claves, booming kick drums, and angelic vocal sighs to play infinitely through the rotating beacon mechanism. Decades later, Arctic king-crab fishermen still claim that whenever the solar flares set the night sky alight, their bridge transceivers pull Torstein's voice out of the empty air: "Drink the kettle down before the beacon drowns." The track endures as a mythic ghost transmission, immortalizing the keepers who turned polar survival into pure acoustic gold.
